\title[Prime-power hits carry the smoothness budget]
{Prime-Power Hits Carry the Smoothness Budget:\\ An Exact Theory of Divisibility Features for Smooth-Number Yield}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{4 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $B \ge 2$ and let $\Psi_B(x)$ denote the number of $B$-smooth integers in $[1,x]$. Sieve-based factoring algorithms are governed by the yield of $B$-smoothness tests, and predicting that yield per instance --- rather than asymptotically --- is a persistent practical problem. We develop an exact structural theory of \emph{divisibility hit features}: indicator statistics of the form ``$m$ divides the candidate'', restricted to the smooth pool.

Our central identity is an exact rescaling theorem: for a positive $B$-smooth modulus $m$, the number of $B$-smooth $v \le x$ with $m \mid v$ equals $\Psi_B(\lfloor x/m\rfloor)$ exactly, with no error term; hence a divisibility feature is not a new arithmetic condition but a change of the smoothness budget. The hypothesis that $m$ be smooth is necessary ($B=5$, $m=7$, $x=100$ gives $0 \ne 10$). From the rescaling we derive: a graded valuation spectrum; an exact budget shift $u(v) = u(v/p^2) + 2\log p/\log B$ on the smoothness parameter, antitone in $B$; a budget decomposition theorem exhibiting the prime-power hit counts as a linear coordinate system for $\sum \Omega(v)$; a completeness theorem for the prime-power hit profile, contrasted with exact blindness and forced pigeonhole collisions for its squarefree truncation; two-sided abundance bounds $(m+1)^{\pi(B)} \le \Psi_B(x) \le (\lfloor \log_2 x\rfloor + 1)^{\pi(B)}$; and an $\FF_2$ blind spot showing that prime-power hits spend smoothness budget without purchasing a relation direction.

These results explain a measured phenomenon: prime-power hit indicators ($p^2 \mid v$, $p \le 13$) add $+0.0892$ out-of-sample $R^2$ (confidence interval $[0.041,0.125]$) over a full baseline containing squarefree-hit features, while mid-prime fractions ($+0.019$) and quadratic-residue density ($+0.004$) have confidence intervals spanning zero. The theorems identify the mechanism as information-theoretic disjointness of two graded layers.
\end{abstract}

